---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import CodeExample from "../../components/CodeExample.astro";
import PageIntro from "../../components/PageIntro.astro";
import { SITE } from "../../consts";
import variablesSource from "../../generated/examples/tutorial-variables.kwe?raw";

const source = variablesSource.trimEnd();
const output = "Uche\n36\neziokwu\nefu\nAda";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Lesson 2: Variables and Values"
  description="Learn Kwechiri variables, reassignment, strings, numbers, booleans, and absence."
>
  <PageIntro
    eyebrow="Tutorial 02"
    title="Variables and Values"
    lead="Store values under names, read them, and update a variable after its first assignment."
    meta="Beginner · Complete Lesson 1 first"
  />

  <div class="shell content-layout">
    <nav class="docs-nav" aria-label="Tutorial navigation">
      <a href="/tutorials/ndewo-uwa/">← Lesson 1: Ndewo Ụwa</a>
      <a href="/tutorials/variables-and-values/" aria-current="page">Lesson 2: Variables and values</a>
      <a href="/tutorials/expressions-and-decisions/">Lesson 3: Expressions and decisions</a>
      <span>Loops · Planned</span>
    </nav>
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>The program</h2>
      <p>Open <code>examples/tutorials/variables.kwe</code>.</p>
      <CodeExample label="examples/tutorials/variables.kwe" code={source} output={output} />

      <h2>Run it</h2>
      <pre><code>node dist/cli/index.js examples/tutorials/variables.kwe</code></pre>

      <h2>What happened</h2>
      <p>The first assignment to a name creates a variable:</p>
      <pre><code>aha = "Uche"</code></pre>
      <p>
        The value on the right is stored under <code>aha</code>. Kwechiri can
        then read <code>aha</code> in later statements. A later assignment
        updates the same variable:
      </p>
      <pre><code>aha = "Ada"</code></pre>

      <div class="table-wrap">
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Value</th><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>"Uche"</code></td><td>string</td><td>Text</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>36</code></td><td>number</td><td>Numeric value</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>eziokwu</code></td><td>boolean</td><td>True</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>efu</code></td><td>absence</td><td>No value</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        Kwechiri does not convert between these types implicitly. The string
        <code>"36"</code> and the number <code>36</code> are different values.
        Variable names are case-sensitive and may use Igbo Unicode letters.
      </p>

      <h2>Your turn</h2>
      <pre><code>obodo = "Aba"
bipụta(obodo)

obodo = "Enugu"
bipụta(obodo)</code></pre>
      <p>Save the program as <code>obodo.kwe</code> and confirm the output:</p>
      <pre><code>Aba
Enugu</code></pre>
      <p>
        Then try reading a new variable before assigning it. The compiler
        reports a located undefined-variable error.
      </p>

      <div class="callout callout--gold">
        <strong>Common problems</strong>
        Assignment uses one <code>=</code>. Names match exactly, including case.
        <code>"eziokwu"</code> is a string because of its quotation marks, while
        <code>eziokwu</code> is a Boolean value.
      </div>

      <h2>Normative references</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/language-spec/variables.md`}>Variables</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/language-spec/types.md`}>Values and types</a></li>
        <li><a href={`${SITE.github}/blob/main/docs/language-spec/cli.md`}>Command-line interface</a></li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Next</h2>
      <p>
        Continue with
        <a href="/tutorials/expressions-and-decisions/">Lesson 3: Expressions and Decisions</a>.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
